WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:08.670
Let's go to the phones at (617) 830-4750 starting
today with our caller from the one seven area

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:09.670
code.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.080
Who's calling today from nine one seven.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.880
Hey David, is this me?

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:14.880
That is you indeed.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:15.880
Who is this

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:16.880
a Milo?

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:17.880
Milo?

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.560
What's going on?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.960
Just to be up.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:21.960
Yeah.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.189
Um, so my question is about Medicare for all.

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:31.090
So I'm a really calm in a right wing and quite
frankly, you know, a talking point also from

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:36.850
like a lot of centrist Democrats is that,
you know, uh, medicare for all really only

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.620
works in countries with like smaller populations.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.079
Like when I bring up that it works in like
Sweden or Norway or Finland or other Nordic

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:49.320
countries, they say like, oh, well, you know,
those countries have like relatively smaller

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.510
populations, but in a country like America
with like 320 million plus people that can't

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.510
work.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.800
So I'm just wondering like, how do you rebut
that point?

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.430
Well, first of all, with 90% of the people
who say that, you just say, why not?

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.540
Why would more people make it not work?

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.560
And usually so that'll get rid of 90% 90%
won't have an answer.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.630
The other 10% will have some answer.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:14.630
And then typically, you know, I mean the,
the best thing is not to rebut it with some

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.460
concocted talking point, but just to tell
the truth, which is the way that insurance

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:24.020
works, the larger the risk pool, the more
predictable the spending is going to be per

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.020
person.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.790
And that actually having more people in the
risk pool makes the system more financially

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:29.790
viable.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:34.100
That you have way more people who are paying
into the system and that it will actually

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.840
make healthcare spending a significantly more
predictable.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.140
And that there is, there's simply no reason
to say that it only works in smaller countries.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.800
I mean it's is mathematically untrue and it's
logically untrue.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.890
Right.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:48.970
Yeah.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:53.280
And also just like sort of one other quick
question, I had one of 'em and I think Kyle

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:02.040
Kalinsky also brought up this point recently,
which is that one sort of I guess a solution

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.810
to that worry or you know, another way we
could sort of do it in America is you sort

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:13.200
of federally mandate that each individual
state implemented like on the state level.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.790
So that population is no longer a worry.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:16.990
I love that.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.630
I've actually mentioned this before.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.340
This is a really good point.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:26.680
If you, if you want to make a state's rights,
sort of positive version of Medicare for all

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:32.840
you actually have each state, you could argue
that states for geographical reasons or because

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.030
some states have these like cancer alley factories
have higher rates of certain conditions.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.420
You can actually say that different states
may actually need slightly different financial,

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:51.240
um, uh, characteristics, and that each state
could implement their own single payer system,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.420
partially subsidized by the federal government
through the sort of taxation that, uh, Bernie

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:57.910
Sanders, for example, has proposed.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.200
That's a fantastic idea.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.200
Right?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.340
All right, well, yeah, thanks for answering
my question, David.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:05.340
All right.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.340
I appreciate the call.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.340
Thank you.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.340
Very, very important stuff.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:08.345
Great to hear from you.

